malisation of the problem**\\nLet $n=2025$.  A placement of uncovered unit squares is a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$: the unique uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.  All other $n^2-n$ unit squares must be covered by axis‑aligned rectangles (tiles) whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\\n\\n**2. Decomposition into left and right parts**\\nFor each row $i$ define\\n\\\\[L_i = p(i)-1,\\\\qquad R_i = n-p(i).\\\\]\\nThe $L_i$ squares to the left of the hole in row $i$ form a contiguous horizontal segment $[1,\\\\,L_i]$, and the $R_i$ squares to the right form $[p(i)+1,\\\\,n]$.  Any rectangle that covers a left‑hand square must have its column interval contained in $[1,\\\\,c]$ for some $c$, otherwise it would intersect the hole $(i,p(i))$.  Consequently every rectangle covering left‑hand squares is of the form\\n\\\\[\\\\text{rows }I\\\\times\\\\text{columns }[1,c],\\\\]\\nwith $c\\\\le L_i$ for every $i\\\\in I$.  In other words such a rectangle is anchored at the left border of the board.\\nSimilarly, every rectangle covering right‑hand squares is anchored at the right border and has the form\\n\\\\[\\\\text{rows }J\\\\times\\\\text{columns }[d,n],\\\\]\\nwith $d\\\\ge p(i)+1$ for all $i\\\\in J$.\\n\\n**3. A lower bound for the left side**\\nThe multiset $\\n\\\\{L_i\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ equals $\\n\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ because $p$ is a permutation.  Hence there are exactly $n-1$ distinct positive values among the $L_i$.\\nConsider any tiling of the left side.  Let $c$ be the largest column index used by a left‑anchored rectangle $R\\\\times[1,c]$.  All rows $i$ belonging to $R$ satisfy $L_i\\\\ge c$.  Remove those rows from further consideration.  The next rectangle must use a smaller column width, because the rows that remain have a strictly smaller maximal $L_i$.  Thus each distinct positive value of $L_i$ forces a new rectangle.  Consequently at least $n-1$ left‑anchored rectangles are necessary.\\n\\n**4. A lower bound for the right side**\\nExactly the same argument applied to the right lengths $R_i=n-p(i)$ (which also form the set $\\n\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$) shows that at least $n-1$ right‑anchored rectangles are required.\\n\\n**5. Global lower bound**\\nSince the left‑anchored and right‑anchored rectangles are disjoint (they lie on opposite sides of the diagonal holes), any tiling of the whole board needs at least\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\]\\nrectangles.\\n\\n**6. Construction attaining the bound**\\nChoose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$, i.e. the uncovered squares are the $n$ diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  For each $k=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ place a vertical rectangle\\n\\\\[\\\\{(i,k)\\\\mid i=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\]\\nwhich covers all squares left of the diagonal in column $k$.  For each $k=2,\\\\dots,n$ place a vertical rectangle\\n\\\\[\\\\{(i,k)\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,k-1\\\\},\\\\]\\nwhich covers all squares right of the diagonal in column $k$.  The two families use disjoint row intervals, so the rectangles do not overlap.  No rectangle contains a diagonal cell because each rectangle stays strictly on one side of the diagonal.  Altogether we have $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ rectangles, and they cover every non‑hole square exactly once.\\n\\n**7. Conclusion**\\nThe lower bound $2n-2$ is achievable, therefore it is optimal.  For $n=2025$ the minimum number of tiles is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\n\\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4040\n}"
