    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is $2n-2$ for an $n\\times n$ board, and for $n=2025$ this equals $4048$.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- Model the uncovered squares as a permutation $p$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$; the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.\n- Split the board into the *left* region $L=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\}$ and the *right* region $R=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}$.  These two regions are disjoint.\n- Show that each of $L$ and $R$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The key lemma is that for $k=1,\\dots,n-1$ the cell $c_k=(r_k,k)$ (where $r_k$ is the unique row with $p(r_k)=k+1$) is the right\u2011most cell of its row in $L$, and any rectangle can contain at most one such $c_k$.  Hence $|L|$ requires at least $n-1$ rectangles; an analogous argument works for $R$.\n- Provide an explicit tiling attaining the bound.  Use the cyclic\u2011shift permutation $p(i)=i+1$ for $i<n$ and $p(n)=1$.  In each row $i$ place a horizontal rectangle covering the left part $\\{(i,1),\\dots,(i,i)\\}$ and, for $i\\ge2$, a horizontal rectangle covering the right part $\\{(i,i+2),\\dots,(i,n)\\}$.  These $2n-2$ rectangles are disjoint, avoid all holes, and cover every other unit square.\n\nThus the lower bound $2n-2$ is tight, giving the answer $\\boxed{4048}$ for the $2025\\times2025$ grid.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**1. Formalisation.**\nLet $n=2025$.  Choose any placement of the $n$ uncovered unit squares; because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  Write $p:\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ for the associated permutation, i.e. the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.\n\n**2. Decomposition into left and right regions.**\nDefine\n$$L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.$$\nEvery unit square that is not a hole belongs either to $L$ or to $R$, and $L\\cap R=\\varnothing$.\nFor each row $i$ set\n$$L_i=p(i)-1,\\qquad R_i=n-p(i).$$\nThus $L_i$ is the number of squares to the left of the hole in row $i$, and $R_i$ the number to its right.  Because $p$ is a permutation, the multiset $\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots,n\\}$ equals $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$, and the same holds for $\\{R_i\\}$.\n\n**3. A lower bound for the left region.**\nFor each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ let $r_k$ be the unique row with $L_{r_k}=k$ (equivalently $p(r_k)=k+1$).  The cell\n$$c_k=(r_k,k)$$\nis the right\u2011most cell of row $r_k$ that lies in $L$.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle that is completely contained in $L$ can contain at most one of the cells $c_1,\\dots,c_{n-1}$.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle $\\mathcal R$ contains $c_a$ and $c_b$ with $a<b$.  Then the column interval of $\\mathcal R$ must contain both columns $a$ and $b$, so its rightmost column is at least $b$.  Because $\\mathcal R\\subseteq L$, every row $i$ used by $\\mathcal R$ satisfies $L_i\\ge b$.  In particular, row $r_a$ (which has $L_{r_a}=a<b$) cannot belong to $\\mathcal R$, contradicting the presence of $c_a$ in $\\mathcal R$.  \u220e\n\nSince the $n-1$ cells $c_k$ are pairwise distinct, Lemma\u00a01 implies that at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover all of $L$.\n\n**4. A lower bound for the right region.**\nDefine $s_k$ as the unique row with $R_{s_k}=k$ (equivalently $p(s_k)=n-k$) and let\n$$d_k=(s_k,n-k)$$\nbe the left\u2011most cell of row $s_k$ that lies in $R$.  An argument identical to Lemma\u00a01 shows that any rectangle contained in $R$ can contain at most one of the cells $d_1,\\dots,d_{n-1}$.  Hence at least $n-1$ rectangles are required for $R$.\n\n**5. Global lower bound.**\nSince $L$ and $R$ are disjoint, the rectangles covering $L$ and those covering $R$ are distinct.  Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least\n$$(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$$\ntiles.\n\n**6. Construction attaining the bound.**\nTake the *cyclic\u2011shift* permutation\n$$p(i)=\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\le i<n,\\\\[2pt]1,&i=n.\\end{cases}$$\nThus the hole in row $i$ is at column $i+1$ for $i<n$, and the hole in the last row is at column $1$.\nFor each $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ place a horizontal $1\\times i$ rectangle covering the cells\n$$\\{(i,1),(i,2),\\dots,(i,i)\\}.$$\nThese $n-1$ rectangles cover the entire left region $L$.\nFor each $i\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$ place a horizontal $1\\times (n-i)$ rectangle covering the cells\n$$\\{(i,i+2),(i,i+3),\\dots,(i,n)\\}.$$\nThese $n-1$ rectangles cover the right region $R$.  The two families are disjoint because they lie in different rows, and none of them contains a hole (the hole in row $i$ is at column $i+1$, which is omitted by both rectangles).  Altogether we have $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles, and they cover every non\u2011hole square exactly once.\n\n**7. Conclusion.**\nThe lower bound $2n-2$ is tight.  For $n=2025$ the minimum possible number of tiles is\n$$2\\cdot2025-2=4048.$$",
    "method": "analysis",
    "final_answer": 4040
}
