d unit square, the set U of uncovered squares is a set of n cells with pairwise distinct rows and distinct columns. Hence there exists a permutation p of {1,…,n} such that\\nU = { (i, p_i ) | i = 1,…,n }.\\n\\n**Lemma 2 (adjacent cells).**\\nFor each i with p_i>1 define the left‑adjacent cell\\nL_i = ( i , p_i−1 ).\\nFor each i with p_i<n define the right‑adjacent cell\\nR_i = ( i , p_i+1 ).\\nExactly one row has p_i=1 and exactly one row has p_i=n, so among the n rows there are (n−1) left‑adjacent cells and (n−1) right‑adjacent cells. Hence the total number of adjacent cells is\\n|A| = (n−1)+(n−1) = 2n−2.\\n\\n**Lemma 3 (a rectangle contains at most one adjacent cell).**\\nConsider any axis‑aligned rectangle T that lies completely in the complement of U. Let its column interval be [c1,c2] with c1 ≤ c2.\\n\\n*Case 1.* Suppose T contains two distinct left‑adjacent cells L_i and L_j with i≠j. Because p_i≠p_j, assume p_i < p_j. Then c1 ≤ p_i−1 and c2 ≥ p_j−1, so c1 ≤ p_i−1 < p_i ≤ c2. Consequently column p_i lies inside [c1,c2]. For row i the cell (i,p_i) belongs to U, contradicting the fact that T is disjoint from U.\\n\\n*Case 2.* The same argument works for two right‑adjacent cells.\\n\\n*Case 3.* Suppose T contains a left‑adjacent cell L_i and a right‑adjacent cell R_j. Without loss of generality p_i ≤ p_j. Then c1 ≤ p_i−1 and c2 ≥ p_j+1, whence c1 ≤ p_i ≤ c2. Again column p_i together with row i yields the uncovered cell (i,p_i)∈U, a contradiction.\\n\\nThus no rectangle can contain two different adjacent cells.\\n\\n**Corollary (lower bound).**\\nSince the 2n−2 adjacent cells are all distinct, any tiling of the complement of U by non‑overlapping rectangles must use at least 2n−2 rectangles.\\n\\n**Construction attaining the bound.**\\nTake the permutation p_i = i (the main diagonal). For each row i:\\n- If i>1, place a 1×(i−1) rectangle covering the cells (i,1),(i,2),…,(i,i−1).\\n- If i<n, place a 1×(n−i) rectangle covering the cells (i,i+1),…,(i,n).\\nThese rect